  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Abu Kersholla Emergency WASH response for returnee and war affected populations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to increase access to WASH services for 11,320 returnees and war-affected populations in Abu Kersholla locality in South Kordofan state.

Following rebel attacks in 2013, there are insufficient water sources and services in the village of Elfaid, with consequent adverse effects on livelihoods and health of the returning population, notably women and children who are traditionally responsible for water collection. They are currently spending 1.5-2 hours per day on water collection rather than education or other domestic activities, and increases in diseases related to poor water quality and sanitation have been recorded. A significant risk of hand pump malfunction exists due to overuse, and local authorities have limited capacity to repair damaged facilities. There is a need to construct water points to provide sufficient safe drinking water for both humans and animals.

The project will provide boreholes, pumps, tanks, troughs and distribution networks; it will provide training in hygiene practices and environmental awareness; and it will facilitate community access to, and management of, adequate sanitation facilities.</narrative>
  </description>
